@@ -0,0 +1,163 @@
+---
+name: draft-check
+description: Run the PeerReviewAgents review engine over the built paper.pdf - specialist tracks only, no reviewer personas and no editor - and write its findings under reviews/ as an actions table. Use to have the review engine audit the built paper, or when asked for an engine, agent or plugin review of the PDF. Needs the peerreview command and a Claude subscription. Read-only.
+context: fork
+agent: general-purpose
+model: sonnet
+---
+
+# Check the built paper with the review engine
+
+Work from the manuscript root and follow AGENTS.md/CLAUDE.md. This skill is
+read-only for the manuscript: it builds nothing but the PDF the engine needs,
+writes one findings file, and never edits prose, `stats.json` or the analysis.
+
+It differs from `/paper:peer-review` in what it is. `peer-review` is this
+session acting as a panel of reviewer personas over the reviewers' copy.
+`draft-check` hands the built PDF to a separate program — the
+PeerReviewAgents engine — which runs its own specialist agents in their own
+sessions, anchors every finding to a quotation in the PDF, and keeps its own
+ledger. The findings come back as somebody else's work, and the value of them
+is exactly that: they were not written by the session that wrote the paper.
+
+## What it needs
+
+1. `peerreview --help` answers. If the command is not on PATH, stop and say
+   so: this skill cannot run without the engine, and nothing else in the
+   scaffold depends on it. Do not pip-install anything.
+2. A Claude Code subscription login on this machine. The engine runs headless
+   sessions of its own; it takes no API key and there is no service to call.
+3. `paper.pdf`, current. Run `just check-build`; if it reports the PDF stale
+   or missing, run `just paper` once and say so in the report. The PDF is an
+   untracked build artifact, so building it is not an edit to the manuscript.
+
+## Read the action ledger first
+
+`reviews/ACTIONS.md` records every finding earlier reviews raised and whether
+it was fixed; its header states the rules. Read it before writing anything.
+If it is missing, create it with `just check-actions --init`. A problem that
+matches an `open`, `done` or `wontfix` row is not a new finding: cite the
+existing id in the findings file instead of raising it again.
+
+If you were launched by `/paper:review-all`, read the ledger to skip what it
+already holds but do not write it — `review-all` merges.
+
+## Run the engine
+
+One command, in the foreground, and only one at a time:
+
+```
+peerreview review paper.pdf reviews/engine/<YYYY-MM-DD> \
+  --arm draft-check --track stats --track consistency --track methods-reporting
+```
+
+* `--arm draft-check` runs the specialist tracks with no core reviewer and no
+  editor. That is the arm an author wants: nothing pools the findings into an
+  accept-or-reject verdict, and every finding stands on its own.
+* The six tracks are `stats`, `figures-tables`, `methods-reporting`,
+  `citations`, `consistency`, `field`. The three above are the default. Add
+  `--track citations` when the request asks about the references; it reaches
+  the network and takes longer. Pass the tracks the user named, and no others.
+* It takes roughly two to six minutes and a few hundred thousand tokens, more
+  with more tracks. Do not run it twice to compare, and do not retry a run
+  that stopped: read what it wrote.
+
+Then read the run, with the engine's own reader rather than by opening files:
+
+```
+peerreview report reviews/engine/<YYYY-MM-DD>
+```
+
+It prints JSON — `status`, `cause`, `findings`, `publishable`,
+`not_publishable_reasons`, `problems` and the report paths — and makes no
+model call.
+
+* `status` `complete` is the result to read. `blocked` means the run stopped
+  (a rate limit, a timeout, a session that never launched); report the
+  `cause`, keep the partial findings out of the ledger, and stop.
+* `problems` naming files means the run folder could not be read in full.
+  Say which, and treat the findings as incomplete evidence.
+* `publishable` will be `false`, and this is correct, not a fault:
+  `not_publishable_reasons` says the findings come from the ledger rather than
+  from an editor's package, and the `draft-check` arm has no editor by design.
+  Do not report it as a failure, and do not gate on it. The gate here is
+  `status` and `problems`.
+
+The findings themselves are `reviews/engine/<YYYY-MM-DD>/review/findings.jsonl`
+(one JSON object per line) with the per-track audits beside them as
+`review/audit_<track>.md`. Read the JSONL for the table and the audits for
+what a track concluded overall.
+
+A finding in `review/quarantined.jsonl` was rejected by the engine's own
+guard, nearly always because its quote does not occur in the block it cites.
+Those are not findings: leave them out of the table and the ledger, and say
+how many there were.
+
+## Translate the findings
+
+Each engine finding carries `title`, `comment`, `quote`, `block_id`,
+`severity` (`major`, `moderate`, `minor`), `kind` (`question`, `edit`,
+`literature`), `track`, `escalate` and a `finding_uid`. Three columns need a
+decision:
+
+**Location.** The engine's `block_id` is a position in the PDF, not a place in
+the Typst sources, and it does not survive a rebuild. Give the location as the
+section heading plus the finding's `quote`, which is the sentence as the PDF
+prints it, and leave the block id to the `source` column. Whoever fixes it
+searches the sources for the quoted words.
+
+**Severity.** The engine's severities are a reviewer's, not the ledger's, and
+it has no `blocker`. Map them so the adapter never invents one:
+
+| engine | ledger |
+|---|---|
+| `major` with `escalate` true | blocker |
+| `major` | major |
+| `moderate` | minor |
+| `minor` | minor |
+
+Say in the verdict paragraph that the mapping is deliberately conservative: a
+`moderate` the author reads as decisive can be promoted by hand, and a reader
+of the ledger can see which rows came through it.
+
+**Fix.** Route by what the finding asks for, to an owner the scaffold already
+has: wording to `/paper:copy-edit`; a number in the text that disagrees with
+another number, or with none declared, to `/paper:declare-number`; a figure or
+table that disagrees with its caption or the text to `/paper:new-figure`; a
+failing gate to `/paper:fix-verify`; a computation the engine says is wrong to
+"analysis change"; a `question` about what was done, and anything on the
+`field` track, to "author decision" with the question stated. A `literature`
+kind is an "author decision" unless it names a citation to add, which is
+`/paper:copy-edit`.
+
+Do not re-judge a finding on its merits and do not add findings of your own.
+If the engine is wrong about something, the ledger row says so in the `fix`
+column as "author decision" with the reason; a disagreement is for the author
+to close, not for this pass to drop silently.
+
+## Report
+
+Write `reviews/<YYYY-MM-DD>-draft-check.md`:
+
+1. A one-paragraph verdict: the arm and tracks, the run folder, how many
+   findings in each mapped severity, how many the guard quarantined, the
+   engine's own token and wall-clock numbers from `review/runtime.json`, and
+   the two or three findings that matter most.
+2. The findings as a table: severity, location (section and quote), the
+   finding, what the engine checked (its track and `kind`), the routed fix,
+   and the `finding_uid` with the run folder as the `source`. The uid names
+   the finding inside that run, and the run folder is what makes it
+   traceable — a later run of a rebuilt PDF gives the same finding a different
+   uid, so a row cites both or neither.
+3. Anything the run could not do: a `blocked` status with its cause, named
+   `problems`, a track that returned nothing.
+
+Then, unless `/paper:review-all` launched you, merge into `reviews/ACTIONS.md`
+exactly as the other review skills do: dedupe against the open and `wontfix`
+rows, reopen a `done` row whose problem is back, append the rest blockers
+first with `status` `open`, an empty `closed`, and `source` citing this
+findings file; then run `just check-actions` and repair any format error in
+the rows you wrote.
+
+Finish by printing the verdict paragraph and the file path. Nothing in the
+manuscript was edited, so do not run `just verify` or `just paper` again.
